// The `hive.ui` pages
// ===================
// A file of its own because the module is: a window, a page, a canvas and a scene,
// each with a vocabulary somebody reaches for on its own. Fourteen pages, so laying
// out a form costs nobody the shape table and the sound rules.
import ./page

// Every page about `hive.ui`.
func pages(): page.Page[dyn] {
    return [
        page.Page("stdlib/ui.md", ui()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-widgets.md", widgets()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-attributes.md", attributes()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-events.md", events()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-enums.md", enums()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-icons.md", icons()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-colour.md", colour()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-canvas.md", canvas()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-code.md", code()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene.md", scene()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene-shapes.md", sceneShapes()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene-surfaces.md", sceneSurfaces()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene-sound.md", sceneSound()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene-camera.md", sceneCamera()),
        page.Page("stdlib/ui-scene-input.md", sceneInput())
    ]
}

// --- The module --------------------------------------------------------------

func ui(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~

        **A view is a value** — a tree of widgets — and what paints it is decided
        somewhere else.

        | call | what it does |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~window(title, view, update)~BT~ | a window, as a handler for ~BT~spawn~BT~ — which opens it |
        | ~BT~webview(title, view, update)~BT~ | the same window, always drawn as a page |
        | ~BT~html(view)~BT~ | renders the tree as an HTML **fragment** → ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~page(title, view)~BT~ | renders it as a whole **document** → ~BT~Str~BT~ |

        ~BT~view~BT~ is a ~BT~func(State): hive.ui.View~BT~ and ~BT~update~BT~ is a
        ~BT~proc(mut State, Msg): Msg~BT~, or ~BT~proc(mut State, Msg, Address<Msg>): Msg~BT~ to be handed
        the window's own address. ~BT~window~BT~ answers with a handler of the same shape, and
        the state a window starts from is what it is spawned with:
        ~BT~spawn(ui.window("Notes", view, update), fresh())~BT~.

        | page | what is on it |
        | --- | --- |
        | [ui-widgets](ui-widgets.md) | every widget and its payload |
        | [ui-attributes](ui-attributes.md) | layout, text, state — what an attribute carries |
        | [ui-events](ui-events.md) | ~BT~on~BT~, ~BT~onInput~BT~, ~BT~onChoose~BT~ and the rest |
        | [ui-enums](ui-enums.md) | ~BT~Align~BT~ ~BT~Justify~BT~ ~BT~TextSize~BT~ ~BT~Axis~BT~ ~BT~Anchor~BT~ ~BT~InputKind~BT~ ~BT~Fit~BT~ |
        | [ui-icons](ui-icons.md) | the 320 ~BT~Icon~BT~s, by name |
        | [ui-colour](ui-colour.md) | ~BT~Tone~BT~ |
        | [ui-canvas](ui-canvas.md) | two dimensions, in the program's own units |
        | [ui-code](ui-code.md) | a code editor: text in colours, a caret its program can move |
        | [ui-scene](ui-scene.md) | three dimensions — **five pages**, start there |

        ## What a window is

        On Windows and Linux, **one the program draws itself** — see below. Elsewhere, an
        **application-mode browser window** — no address bar and no tabs — running
        against a **profile of its own**, so it is its own process rather than a window
        inside somebody's browser: none of your own session, extensions or history is in
        it, and quitting a browser does not close it. A machine with no Chromium-family
        browser falls back to an ordinary tab.

        **On Windows and Linux the program draws its window itself**, with no browser
        anywhere: every widget has a version of its own there, laid out by the
        stylesheet's own rules and light or dark as the desktop is. Linux speaks X11,
        which a Wayland desktop serves through XWayland, and sets the system's own
        sans-serif. A ~BT~scene~BT~ there is drawn by its engine, started beside the
        program. A window opened with ~BT~webview~BT~ keeps the page, and so does a
        program shipping a typeface of its own, a Linux machine with no display or no
        usable font, macOS and Android. The layout is modelled on
        [Clay](https://github.com/nicbarker/clay), by Nic Barker.

        **It behaves as a page would**: text is selected and copied, a right click opens a
        menu, a field undoes, scroll bars drag, a screen reader reads it (UI Automation on
        Windows, AT-SPI on Linux), and on Linux IBus or Fcitx5 types into it. An ~BT~image~BT~
        may be PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP or SVG.

        **Opening one does not wait for it**, so a program may open as many as it likes.
        Closing a window kills its service, and **closing the last one ends the
        program**. A ~BT~main~BT~ that returns while a window is open waits for them all;
        [~BT~hive.task.sleepForever~BT~](task.md) says the same for one with nothing left to
        do.

        On Windows a **built** windowed program carries no console, so what opens is the
        window and nothing else. ~BT~hive run~BT~ keeps one, because that is where the
        program's own output belongs while you are writing it.

        ## Its icon is ~BT~assets/icon.png~BT~

        A PNG of at most 256×256, beside the entrypoint. The build **embeds it into the
        executable** — the window links it, and on Windows the file carries it as a
        resource, so a file manager and a pinned shortcut show it too. A window that
        ships none shows Hive's own mark.

        It is **the program's** icon, not the window's: any program may ship one, and a
        Windows build carries it whether a window ever opens or not.

        ~CODE~
        my-app/
          app.hive
          assets/icon.png     the window's icon, and the executable's
        ~CODE~

        **Nothing in the source names it.** What a window shows and what the file
        carries have to be one image, so there is one place to put it.

        ## Its typeface is ~BT~assets/font.woff2~BT~

        By the same convention: ship the file and every widget is set in it; ship none
        and the window uses the system's own. Nothing in the source names it either.

        ## A finger is not a mouse

        Where the pointer is coarse, a button, a field and a checkbox stand **at least
        44px tall** and a field's text is **16px** — below which a phone zooms the page on
        focus and never zooms back. **No layout moves:** a ~BT~row~BT~ is a row at every width,
        on every device.

        ## Two environment variables

        | variable | does |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~HIVE_WINDOW=print~BT~ | prints ~BT~hive-window <url>~BT~ instead of opening a browser |
        | ~BT~HIVE_FOLDS=1~BT~ | prints, once a second, turns taken and drawn, the median, 90th-percentile and worst time in ~BT~update~BT~, ~BT~view~BT~ and sending the page, and the collections since |

        An app from ~BT~hive export~BT~ takes ~BT~HIVE_FOLDS~BT~ as an intent extra — see
        [cli](../cli.md).

        A window the program draws itself usually has no console to print a crash
        to, so **what a crash prints is also appended to
        ~BT~<temp>/<program>-crash.log~BT~** — the file to look in when one closes by itself.

        ## The window is a service

        ~BT~update~BT~ is **a [service's](syslink.md) handler**, and is checked as one, and a
        window is started by ~BT~spawn~BT~ like any other service. So a window **has an
        address**, may be spawned under a name, and can be posted to by a background task
        or by another machine. That is why the module needs no notion of a *command*.

        ## The view is a ~BT~func~BT~, and that is not a formality

        **A ~BT~func~BT~ cannot take a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter, so drawing cannot write the model** — or
        anything else the caller can see — which matters because a repaint happens on
        every change.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Msg \{
        	Typed \{ text: Str }
        	Send
        }

        type State \{
        	draft: Str
        	lines: Str[dyn]
        }

        func view(state: State): hive.ui.View \{
        	return ui.column([ui.gap(8), ui.pad(16)], [
        		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title())], "Notes"),
        		ui.input([ui.onInput(Msg.Typed(_)), ui.placeholder("a line")], state.draft),
        		ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Send())], "Add")
        	])
        }

        proc update(state: mut State, message: Msg): Msg \{
        	if message is Msg.Typed(text) \{
        		state.draft = text
        	} else if message is Msg.Send \{
        		append(state.lines, state.draft)
        		state.draft = ""
        	}
        	return message
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	Str[dyn] empty = []
        	spawn(ui.window("Notes", view, update), State("", empty))
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## One tree, two destinations

        **The same tree a window swaps in is what ~BT~page~BT~ writes out**, so a view built
        for a window is a view an ~BT~httpServe~BT~ handler can answer with, unchanged:

        ~CODE~hive
        proc handle(req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
        	return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], ui.page("Notes", view(state)))
        }
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~link~BT~ is the one widget that works without an event: a window follows the
        **message** and a served page follows the **href**, from the identical tree.

        ## Why the widget list is short

        **A widget is here only if it cannot be composed from the others *and* needs
        something the renderer has that Hive does not.** A card is a ~BT~column~BT~ with
        padding, a toast is an ~BT~overlay~BT~ and a message, a divider is a ~BT~row~BT~ with a
        border. Do not go looking for those.
    `)
}

func widgets(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ — the widgets

        **Every widget takes the same two things: its attributes, then its payload.**
        There are no optional parameters in Hive, so what would be an optional argument
        elsewhere is an entry in the attribute vector.

        | widget | payload |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~row(attrs, children)~BT~ / ~BT~column(attrs, children)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~grid(attrs, children)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ — [columns that line up](#a-grid-and-a-stack) |
        | ~BT~stack(attrs, children)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ — laid one over another |
        | ~BT~paragraph(attrs, children)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ — [words that wrap as one](#a-paragraph) |
        | ~BT~spacer()~BT~ | — takes **nothing at all**, not even attributes |
        | ~BT~none()~BT~ | — the same; draws nothing |
        | ~BT~spinner(attrs)~BT~ | — attributes only |
        | ~BT~progress(attrs, done)~BT~ | ~BT~Float~BT~ — how much is done, ~BT~0.0~BT~ to ~BT~1.0~BT~ |
        | ~BT~overlay(attrs, child)~BT~ | ~BT~View~BT~ |
        | ~BT~text(attrs, content)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~image(attrs, src, alt)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~link(attrs, href, label)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~icon(attrs, name)~BT~ | ~BT~Icon~BT~ — [the icons](ui-icons.md) |
        | ~BT~button(attrs, label)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~input(attrs, value)~BT~ / ~BT~textarea(attrs, value)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~checkbox(attrs, label, checked)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Bool~BT~ |
        | ~BT~select(attrs, options, chosen)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[], Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~radio(attrs, options, chosen)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[], Str~BT~ — one of several, all in view |
        | ~BT~choices(attrs, options, chosen)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[], Str[]~BT~ — any number of several |
        | ~BT~tabs(attrs, labels, chosen)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[], Str~BT~ — a strip of tabs |
        | ~BT~tree(attrs, branches)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ — [a tree of ~BT~branch~BT~es](#a-tree) |
        | ~BT~branch(attrs, label, open, children)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Bool, View[]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~table(attrs, rows)~BT~ | ~BT~Table~BT~ |
        | ~BT~canvas(attrs, marks)~BT~ | ~BT~Mark[]~BT~ — [ui-canvas](ui-canvas.md) |
        | ~BT~code(attrs, text, runs)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Run[]~BT~ — [ui-code](ui-code.md) |
        | ~BT~scene(attrs, shapes)~BT~ | ~BT~Shape[]~BT~ — [ui-scene](ui-scene.md) |
        | ~BT~inset(attrs, views)~BT~ | ~BT~View[]~BT~ — a second look at that scene |

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.row([ui.gap(12), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
        	ui.icon([], ui.Icon.Star()),
        	ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "starred"),
        	ui.spacer(),
        	ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Clear()), ui.disabled(len(items) == 0)], "Clear")
        ])
        ~CODE~

        A widget's value is a ~BT~hive.ui.View~BT~, which is **opaque**: build it with these
        calls, write it down as a type, and hand it on.

        ## ~BT~spacer~BT~ against ~BT~grow~BT~ against ~BT~none~BT~

        ~BT~spacer()~BT~ eats the leftover room in a ~BT~row~BT~ or a ~BT~column~BT~, which is what
        pushes what follows it to the far end. ~BT~ui.grow(n)~BT~ is an
        [attribute](ui-attributes.md) that gives a **child** a share of it. ~BT~none()~BT~
        draws nothing and is what a branch returns when there is nothing to show —
        Hive has no optional view.

        ## A grid and a stack

        A **~BT~grid~BT~** lays its children into columns, left to right and then down, and
        **every child in a column is as wide as the column**. ~BT~columns(Int[])~BT~ says how
        many there are and how wide: **~BT~0~BT~ is as wide as what is in it, and any other
        number is that many shares of what is left** — ~BT~columns([0, 1])~BT~ is a column of
        labels as wide as the longest, and a column of fields taking the rest. A grid
        saying nothing has one column. ~BT~span(n)~BT~ on a child takes that many columns, and
        ~BT~gap~BT~ is the space both between columns and between rows.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.grid([ui.columns([0, 1]), ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
        	ui.text([], "Name"), ui.input([ui.onInput(Msg.Name(_))], state.name),
        	ui.text([], "Email"), ui.input([ui.kind(ui.InputKind.Email())], state.email),
        	ui.button([ui.span(2), ui.on(Msg.Save())], "Save")
        ])
        ~CODE~

        A **~BT~stack~BT~** lays its children **one over another**, each the size of the
        stack unless it says otherwise: ~BT~place(across, down)~BT~ — two ~BT~Align~BT~s — puts a
        child at an edge or in the middle of it, and ~BT~Stretch~BT~ runs it the whole way.
        It is how a caption goes over a picture, or a badge on the corner of a card.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.stack([ui.aspect(16, 9)], [
        	ui.image([ui.fit(ui.Fit.Cover())], "/_hive/asset/beach.jpg", "a beach"),
        	ui.text([ui.place(ui.Align.End(), ui.Align.Start()), ui.background(ui.Tone.Danger())], "new")
        ])
        ~CODE~

        **~BT~place~BT~ and ~BT~span~BT~ speak to a grid or a stack and to nothing else**: a child
        of a row is placed by the row's own ~BT~align~BT~ and ~BT~justify~BT~.

        ## A paragraph

        A ~BT~paragraph~BT~'s ~BT~text~BT~s and ~BT~link~BT~s **wrap as one run of words**, each in its
        own weight, style, colour or background, and an ~BT~icon~BT~ stands in the line as a
        letter would. Any other widget sits in the line as one block. A ~BT~text~BT~ in a
        paragraph is never a heading.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.paragraph([], [
        	ui.text([], "Read "),
        	ui.link([ui.on(Msg.Terms())], "/terms", "the terms"),
        	ui.text([], " before you sign — "),
        	ui.text([ui.weight(700)], "they changed on Monday"),
        	ui.text([], ".")
        ])
        ~CODE~

        ## Several to choose from

        ~BT~select~BT~, ~BT~radio~BT~ and ~BT~tabs~BT~ each hold **one** choice and report the new one
        through ~BT~onChoose~BT~; ~BT~choices~BT~ holds **any number** and reports the option that
        was clicked, chosen or not, so the program adds it to its set or takes it out.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.radio([ui.onChoose(Msg.Size(_))], ["Small", "Medium", "Large"], state.size)
        ui.choices([ui.onChoose(Msg.Flip(_))], ["red", "green", "blue"], state.colours)
        ui.tabs([ui.onChoose(Msg.Page(_))], ["Profile", "Billing", "Team"], state.page)
        ~CODE~

        A radio group stands in a column, or with ~BT~wrap(true)~BT~ in lines that wrap.
        ~BT~tabs~BT~ is only the strip: what is under it is the program's, chosen by the same
        state. ~BT~progress(attrs, done)~BT~ is a bar as wide as it is given, ~BT~done~BT~ of the way
        along; what is waiting with no measure of how far it has come is a ~BT~spinner~BT~.

        ## A tree

        A ~BT~tree~BT~ holds ~BT~branch~BT~es, and a branch holds more: ~BT~branch(attrs, label, open,
        children)~BT~ shows its children only when ~BT~open~BT~ is true. **Its row is clicked
        with ~BT~on~BT~, and its arrow opens and closes it with ~BT~onToggle~BT~**, which says
        what the branch would become. ~BT~selected(true)~BT~ marks the one the program has
        picked.

        ~CODE~hive
        func folderView(f: Folder): ui.View \{
        	mut ui.View[dyn] kids = []
        	for each child in f.children \{
        		append(kids, folderView(child))
        	}
        	return ui.branch([ui.on(Msg.Pick(f.id)), ui.onToggle(Msg.Fold(f.id, _)),
        		ui.selected(f.id == picked)], f.name, f.open, kids)
        }
        ~CODE~

        The arrow keys open (**Right**) and close (**Left**) the focused branch, and
        **Enter** picks it.

        ## An ~BT~overlay~BT~ that says where it goes is not a dialog

        * With **neither ~BT~align~BT~ nor ~BT~justify~BT~** it is centred over a dimmed page,
          which is what a dialog is.
        * With **either of them** it is pinned to that edge instead: nothing is dimmed,
          and **clicks pass through everywhere the panel is not** — which is what a
          heads-up display in a corner, a toast, or a legend over a picture needs.

        * With **~BT~anchor~BT~** it opens where the pointer was last pressed
          (~BT~Anchor.Pointer~BT~) or under the caret of the code editor being typed in
          (~BT~Anchor.Caret~BT~), above it where there is no room below. Nothing is dimmed,
          and **a press anywhere else is its ~BT~onDismiss~BT~** — which is what a context
          menu and a list of completions need.

        A pinned panel keeps none of the dialog's own shape either: no rounded corner,
        no shadow, and no cap on its height, so ~BT~grow~BT~ and ~BT~align(Stretch)~BT~ will run
        one the full width or height of the window. Something laid over a part of the
        window rather than the whole of it is a ~BT~stack~BT~.

        ## A button or a field in a colour of its own

        A ~BT~button~BT~, ~BT~input~BT~, ~BT~textarea~BT~, ~BT~select~BT~ or ~BT~code~BT~ given a ~BT~background~BT~
        draws **no edge of its own** unless it asks for one with ~BT~border~BT~ or ~BT~borderTone~BT~ —
        the edge was there to set the default look off from the page — and a button in
        a colour of its own lights up under the pointer rather than taking the accent.
        ~BT~radius(n)~BT~ rounds it (a button is a pill until told otherwise), and
        ~BT~ring(false)~BT~ keeps the accent ring off while it has the focus.

        ## ~BT~link~BT~ works without an event

        The window follows the **message** and a served page follows the **href**, from
        the identical tree. **Where a link may point is a closed set** — a path within
        the page, a relative path, or ~BT~http~BT~, ~BT~https~BT~, ~BT~mailto~BT~ — and anything else
        renders as no destination at all.

        ## What a widget holds is what the state says

        ~BT~select~BT~, ~BT~checkbox~BT~, ~BT~radio~BT~, ~BT~choices~BT~, ~BT~tabs~BT~ and ~BT~branch~BT~ take **what is
        currently chosen or open** as their payload, because a view is a function of
        the state: each redraws with whatever the state now says, and the
        [event](ui-events.md) is what changed the state. Nothing is remembered inside a
        widget.
    `)
}

func attributes(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ — the attributes

        **Attributes are semantic tokens rather than CSS values**, and each is a call
        answering with a ~BT~hive.ui.Attr~BT~. They go in the vector every widget takes
        first. Events have a page of their own: [ui-events](ui-events.md).

        | attribute | carries |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~gap(n)~BT~ ~BT~pad(n)~BT~ ~BT~width(n)~BT~ ~BT~height(n)~BT~ ~BT~grow(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ |
        | ~BT~minWidth(n)~BT~ ~BT~maxWidth(n)~BT~ ~BT~minHeight(n)~BT~ ~BT~maxHeight(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — [the limits of a size](#limits-and-a-shape) |
        | ~BT~aspect(wide, tall)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int~BT~ — a box as many wide as it is tall |
        | ~BT~align(Align)~BT~ ~BT~justify(Justify)~BT~ | an [enumeration](ui-enums.md) |
        | ~BT~wrap(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — a row's children, or a text's words, run on to another line |
        | ~BT~columns(widths)~BT~ ~BT~span(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int[]~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~ — a [grid](ui-widgets.md#a-grid-and-a-stack)'s columns, and how many a child takes |
        | ~BT~place(across, down)~BT~ | ~BT~Align, Align~BT~ — where a child of a grid or a stack stands in its room |
        | ~BT~scroll(Axis)~BT~ | ~BT~Axis~BT~ |
        | ~BT~scrollTo(x, y, serial)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — scroll a box to a place whenever the number changes |
        | ~BT~anchor(Anchor)~BT~ | ~BT~Anchor~BT~ — an overlay that opens at the pointer or the caret |
        | ~BT~size(TextSize)~BT~ | ~BT~TextSize~BT~ |
        | ~BT~heading(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the heading level |
        | ~BT~weight(n)~BT~ ~BT~italic(b)~BT~ ~BT~mono(b)~BT~ ~BT~lines(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~ — [a text's face and length](#text) |
        | ~BT~tone(Tone)~BT~ ~BT~background(Tone)~BT~ ~BT~paint(Tone)~BT~ | [~BT~Tone~BT~](ui-colour.md) |
        | ~BT~gradient(from, to, angle)~BT~ | ~BT~Tone, Tone, Float~BT~ — a fill that runs from one tone to another |
        | ~BT~opacity(f)~BT~ ~BT~shadow(n)~BT~ ~BT~ease(ms)~BT~ | ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~ — [how a box looks, and how it changes](#how-a-box-looks) |
        | ~BT~kind(InputKind)~BT~ | ~BT~InputKind~BT~ |
        | ~BT~limits(low, high, step)~BT~ | ~BT~Float~BT~ — what a slider or a number runs between, and its step |
        | ~BT~fit(Fit)~BT~ | ~BT~Fit~BT~ — how a picture fills its box |
        | ~BT~placeholder(text)~BT~ ~BT~hint(text)~BT~ ~BT~title(text)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~touch(name)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — a thumb control, heard as pad -1 by a scene's ~BT~onPad~BT~ |
        | ~BT~keys(names)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ — the keys its [~BT~onKey~BT~](ui-events.md) takes |
        | ~BT~carry(word)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — what a widget carries when it is dragged onto an [~BT~onDrop~BT~](ui-events.md) |
        | ~BT~selected(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — a [branch](ui-widgets.md#a-tree) the program has picked |
        | ~BT~caret(anchor, head, serial)~BT~ ~BT~tabSize(n)~BT~ ~BT~fontSize(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — a [code editor](ui-code.md)'s |
        | ~BT~border(n)~BT~ ~BT~radius(n)~BT~ ~BT~borderTone(Tone)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Tone~BT~ — a box's edge: how thick (0: none), how round, in what |
        | ~BT~ring(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — ~BT~false~BT~ hides the accent ring a button or a field shows while it has the focus |
        | ~BT~hover(Tone)~BT~ | ~BT~Tone~BT~ — the colour behind any widget while the pointer is over it; a link with one is not underlined |
        | ~BT~numbers(b)~BT~ ~BT~readOnly(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — a code editor's line numbers, and whether it can be typed in |
        | ~BT~perform(command, serial)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Int~BT~ — a code editor does undo, copy, paste… once per serial |
        | ~BT~focus(serial)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — focus this widget (or the first field in it) whenever the number changes |
        | ~BT~clip(text, serial)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Int~BT~ — put ~BT~text~BT~ on the clipboard whenever the number changes |
        | ~BT~disabled(b)~BT~ ~BT~busy(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
        | ~BT~region(x, y, width, height)~BT~ ~BT~thick(w)~BT~ | ~BT~Float~BT~ — a [canvas](ui-canvas.md) |
        | ~BT~edge(Tone)~BT~ ~BT~dash(on, off)~BT~ ~BT~crop(x, y, width, height)~BT~ | a [canvas mark](ui-canvas.md)'s |
        | ~BT~at(x, y, z)~BT~ ~BT~turn(x, y, z)~BT~ ~BT~scale(x, y, z)~BT~ | ~BT~Float~BT~ — a [shape](ui-scene-shapes.md) |
        | ~BT~surface(Surface)~BT~ | a [finish](ui-scene-surfaces.md) |
        | ~BT~voice(Voice)~BT~ ~BT~pitch(f)~BT~ ~BT~volume(f)~BT~ ~BT~track(path)~BT~ | a [sound](ui-scene-sound.md) |
        | ~BT~eye(x, y, z)~BT~ ~BT~aim(yaw, pitch)~BT~ ~BT~lens(deg)~BT~ ~BT~fog(m)~BT~ | a [camera](ui-scene-camera.md) |
        | ~BT~sun(yaw, pitch)~BT~ ~BT~cast(m)~BT~ | [the sun](ui-scene-camera.md): where it stands, how far it casts |
        | ~BT~shadows(b)~BT~ ~BT~grab(b)~BT~ ~BT~crosshair(b)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — a scene |
        | ~BT~grain(px)~BT~ ~BT~bits(n)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — a scene [drawn coarsely](ui-scene-camera.md) |

        **Watch the number type.** ~BT~gap~BT~, ~BT~pad~BT~, ~BT~width~BT~, ~BT~height~BT~, ~BT~grow~BT~, the four
        limits, ~BT~aspect~BT~, ~BT~columns~BT~, ~BT~span~BT~, ~BT~weight~BT~, ~BT~lines~BT~, ~BT~shadow~BT~, ~BT~ease~BT~,
        ~BT~heading~BT~, ~BT~lens~BT~, ~BT~fog~BT~, ~BT~cast~BT~, ~BT~grain~BT~ and ~BT~bits~BT~ take an ~BT~Int~BT~; everything
        geometric — ~BT~at~BT~, ~BT~turn~BT~, ~BT~eye~BT~, ~BT~aim~BT~, ~BT~region~BT~, ~BT~thick~BT~, ~BT~pitch~BT~, ~BT~volume~BT~,
        ~BT~opacity~BT~, ~BT~limits~BT~, ~BT~gradient~BT~'s angle — takes a ~BT~Float~BT~. Hive never widens a
        number, so ~BT~ui.at(0, 1, 0)~BT~ is a compile error and ~BT~ui.at(0.0, 1.0, 0.0)~BT~ is not.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.column([ui.gap(8), ui.pad(16), ui.scroll(ui.Axis.Vertical())], [
        	ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title()), ui.heading(1)], "Colonies"),
        	ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "\{len(rows)} of \{total}"),
        	ui.table([ui.grow(1)], rows)
        ])
        ~CODE~

        ## Layout

        ~BT~gap~BT~ is the space **between** a ~BT~row~BT~'s or ~BT~column~BT~'s children; ~BT~pad~BT~ is the
        space **inside** its own edge. ~BT~align~BT~ places children across the axis and
        ~BT~justify~BT~ along it. ~BT~grow(n)~BT~ gives a child a **share** of the leftover room —
        two children at ~BT~grow(1)~BT~ take half each — and ~BT~scroll~BT~ says which way a box
        may be scrolled when its contents do not fit.

        ~BT~width~BT~ and ~BT~height~BT~ are a fixed size; ~BT~align(ui.Align.Stretch())~BT~ is how a
        child fills the cross axis instead.

        **~BT~width~BT~ on a ~BT~row~BT~ or a ~BT~column~BT~ is a width, not a floor**: the box is never wider
        than the space it is given, so ~BT~width(460)~BT~ is 460 where there is room and the
        screen's width where there is not. A ~BT~canvas~BT~, a ~BT~scene~BT~ and an ~BT~image~BT~ keep the
        width they asked for, since one wider than its box is what ~BT~scroll(Horizontal)~BT~
        is for.

        **~BT~wrap(true)~BT~ on a row** starts a new line wherever the next child would not
        fit, every line packed to the start and as tall as its tallest child, with ~BT~gap~BT~
        between lines as well as between children.

        In a [grid](ui-widgets.md#a-grid-and-a-stack), ~BT~align~BT~ places each child up and
        down within its row and ~BT~justify~BT~ across its column; a child fills its cell
        until told otherwise.

        ## Limits and a shape

        ~BT~minWidth~BT~, ~BT~maxWidth~BT~, ~BT~minHeight~BT~ and ~BT~maxHeight~BT~ hold a box inside a range
        however it was sized — by its content, its ~BT~width~BT~, a ~BT~grow~BT~ or a stretch — and
        **a least size wins over a greatest one**. ~BT~aspect(16, 9)~BT~ makes a box whose
        height follows its width; what it holds can still make it taller unless it
        scrolls. On an ~BT~image~BT~, ~BT~fit(Fit)~BT~ says what fills the box: ~BT~Fill~BT~ stretches the
        picture to it (and is what an image with a ~BT~width~BT~ and a ~BT~height~BT~ always did),
        ~BT~Contain~BT~ shows all of it, ~BT~Cover~BT~ fills the box and loses the overhang.

        ## Text

        ~BT~size~BT~ picks a role and ~BT~heading~BT~ a level; on top of them, ~BT~weight(n)~BT~ sets how
        heavy the face is, from ~BT~100~BT~ to ~BT~900~BT~ — ~BT~700~BT~ is bold — ~BT~italic(true)~BT~ slants
        it, and ~BT~mono(true)~BT~ sets it in the window's monospaced face. Any of them on a
        container reaches everything in it.

        ~BT~justify~BT~ on a ~BT~text~BT~ or a ~BT~paragraph~BT~ lines its words up: ~BT~Start~BT~, ~BT~Center~BT~,
        ~BT~End~BT~, or ~BT~Between~BT~, which **justifies** — every line but a paragraph's last
        spread to both edges. ~BT~lines(n)~BT~ keeps at most that many lines and **ends the
        last with an ellipsis** where the text went on; ~BT~wrap(false)~BT~ keeps the words on
        the lines the text's own line breaks make.

        ## How a box looks

        ~BT~background~BT~ fills a box with a tone and ~BT~gradient(from, to, angle)~BT~ with two,
        running **in the direction of the angle, in radians: nought is left to right,
        and a positive angle turns clockwise** — ~BT~gradient(a, b, 1.5708)~BT~ runs top to
        bottom. ~BT~border~BT~, ~BT~borderTone~BT~ and ~BT~radius~BT~ draw and round the edge of **any**
        box; a border with no tone of its own is the colour of the box's text.
        ~BT~shadow(n)~BT~ lifts it ~BT~n~BT~ pixels off the page, ~BT~opacity(f)~BT~ makes it and
        everything in it see-through, from ~BT~0.0~BT~ to ~BT~1.0~BT~.

        **~BT~ease(ms)~BT~ makes a change arrive over that many milliseconds** rather than at
        once: the box's colours, opacity, shadow, and a ~BT~width~BT~ or ~BT~height~BT~ it was given
        move from what they were toward what the newest view says, on the curve CSS
        calls ~BT~ease~BT~. Where a box first appears it appears; only a change eases.

        ## State

        ~BT~disabled~BT~ takes a ~BT~Bool~BT~ rather than being present or absent, so it is
        written straight off the state — ~BT~ui.disabled(len(items) == 0)~BT~ — and there is
        no branch to build a different attribute vector.

        ~BT~busy~BT~ says a widget is waiting; ~BT~placeholder~BT~ is the ghost text in an empty
        ~BT~input~BT~. **~BT~hint~BT~ is a tooltip**: the note any widget shows when the pointer
        rests on it. ~BT~kind~BT~ says what an ~BT~input~BT~ is for, which is what gets a date
        picker, a colour picker, a file chooser, a hidden password, or the keyboard a
        phone shows for an email, a telephone number or an address.

        ~BT~title~BT~ on the outermost widget is what the window's title bar says, and it
        follows the state like everything else — ~BT~ui.title("count \{n}")~BT~. Below the
        outermost widget it says nothing; a window whose view gives none keeps the title
        it was opened with.

        ~BT~kind(ui.InputKind.Range())~BT~ is a **slider**, whose value and ~BT~onInput~BT~ text are
        a number. It runs from 0 to 100 a whole number at a time unless
        **~BT~limits(low, high, step)~BT~** says otherwise; a step of ~BT~0.0~BT~ takes any value
        between. The same limits hold a ~BT~Number~BT~ field and the steps its arrows take.
        While a slider has focus it keeps only the keys that move it, so a scene beside
        it still hears ~BT~escape~BT~.

        ~BT~scrollTo(x, y, serial)~BT~ scrolls a box that scrolls to that place, in pixels,
        whenever the number changes and the first time it is seen — which is how a log
        stays at its newest line and a list returns to where it was.

        ## Colour

        ~BT~tone~BT~ is the **foreground** of text, ~BT~background~BT~ is a box's fill, and ~BT~paint~BT~
        is what a canvas mark or a scene shape is coloured. All three take a
        [~BT~Tone~BT~](ui-colour.md).
    `)
}

func events(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ — the events

        An event is an attribute like any other. **Some carry the message itself; the
        rest carry a function of what the user did.**

        | attribute | hands your function |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~on(Msg)~BT~ | — the message itself |
        | ~BT~onDismiss(Msg)~BT~ | — the message itself |
        | ~BT~onInput(f)~BT~ ~BT~onSubmit(f)~BT~ ~BT~onChoose(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~onKey(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — a key a widget took with ~BT~keys~BT~ |
        | ~BT~onEdit(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int, Str~BT~ — a [code editor](ui-code.md)'s edit |
        | ~BT~onCaret(f)~BT~ ~BT~onView(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int~BT~ — its selection, and the lines in view |
        | ~BT~onHover(f)~BT~ ~BT~onJump(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the character under the pointer, and under a Ctrl+click |
        | ~BT~onFit(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int~BT~ — the columns and rows that fit in a code editor |
        | ~BT~onProbe(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the character of a code editor under the pointer while Ctrl is held, -1 when it is let go |
        | ~BT~onPaste(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the clipboard's text, on Ctrl+V in a widget that takes it |
        | ~BT~onMenu(Msg)~BT~ ~BT~onMiddle(Msg)~BT~ ~BT~onDouble(Msg)~BT~ | — a right click, a middle click, a double click |
        | ~BT~onEnter(Msg)~BT~ ~BT~onLeave(Msg)~BT~ | — the pointer coming over a widget, and going off it |
        | ~BT~onFocus(Msg)~BT~ ~BT~onBlur(Msg)~BT~ | — the focus arriving in a widget or anything in it, and leaving |
        | ~BT~onPress(f)~BT~ ~BT~onPoint(f)~BT~ ~BT~onRelease(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Float, Float~BT~ — [where the pointer is](#where-the-pointer-is) when pressed, as it moves, when let go |
        | ~BT~onWheel(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Float, Float~BT~ — how far the wheel turned, across and down, in pixels |
        | ~BT~onDrag(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int, Bool~BT~ — how far since the press, and whether it was let go |
        | ~BT~onScroll(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int~BT~ — how far a box that scrolls is scrolled |
        | ~BT~onSize(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Int~BT~ — how wide and how tall a widget landed, in pixels |
        | ~BT~onDrop(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — what was [carried](#carrying-and-dropping) onto it |
        | ~BT~onFile(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str, Str~BT~ — [a file's name and its bytes](#a-file) |
        | ~BT~onToggle(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
        | ~BT~onPick(f)~BT~ ~BT~onSort(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ |
        | ~BT~onKeyDown(f)~BT~ ~BT~onKeyUp(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — a [scene](ui-scene-input.md) |
        | ~BT~onFrame(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — a scene |
        | ~BT~onLook(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Float, Float~BT~ — a scene |
        | ~BT~onGrab(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — a scene |
        | ~BT~onPad(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int, Str, Float~BT~ — a scene |
        | ~BT~onRate(f)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — a scene |

        ## A constructor with a hole is the function

        This is what [partial application](../expressions.md) is for. ~BT~Msg.Changed(_)~BT~
        is already a ~BT~func(Str): Msg~BT~, so nothing has to be written to adapt it:

        ~CODE~hive
        type Msg \{
        	Typed   \{ text: Str }
        	Toggled \{ done: Bool }
        	Chose   \{ option: Str }
        	Send
        }

        ui.input([ui.onInput(Msg.Typed(_))], state.draft)
        ui.checkbox([ui.onToggle(Msg.Toggled(_))], "done", state.done)
        ui.select([ui.onChoose(Msg.Chose(_))], options, state.chosen)
        ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Send())], "Send")
        ~CODE~

        **The shape must match**: ~BT~onToggle~BT~ is handed a ~BT~Bool~BT~, so its function has to
        take one, and the compiler says so where it does not. An event reporting two
        values wants a **two-hole** constructor —
        ~BT~ui.onLook(Msg.Looked(_, _))~BT~ — and one reporting three wants three.

        Where a message needs something the event does not report, fill the rest in at
        the call site: ~BT~ui.onToggle(Msg.Set(item.id, _))~BT~ captures the id where it was
        written.

        ## What each one is on

        ~BT~on~BT~ is a ~BT~button~BT~ being pressed, a ~BT~link~BT~ being followed, or a ~BT~branch~BT~'s row
        being clicked. ~BT~onDismiss~BT~ is an ~BT~overlay~BT~ being closed. ~BT~onInput~BT~ fires as an
        ~BT~input~BT~ or ~BT~textarea~BT~ changes and ~BT~onSubmit~BT~ when one is entered. ~BT~onChoose~BT~ is
        a ~BT~select~BT~, a ~BT~radio~BT~ group or a strip of ~BT~tabs~BT~ moving to an option, and a
        ~BT~choices~BT~ list's option being clicked. ~BT~onToggle~BT~ is a ~BT~checkbox~BT~, or a
        ~BT~branch~BT~ opening (~BT~true~BT~) or closing. ~BT~onPick~BT~ and ~BT~onSort~BT~ are a ~BT~table~BT~ — a
        row chosen, and a column asked to order by, each **by index**.

        **The rest are on any widget.**

        ## Keys a widget takes for itself

        ~BT~keys(["Ctrl+S", "Escape"])~BT~ says which keys a widget takes, and ~BT~onKey(f)~BT~
        hears them. **The innermost widget around the focus that names a key gets it**,
        and nothing else happens to it — the field does not type it, Tab does not move
        the focus, the window does not close. A key no widget names goes on as usual.
        With nothing focused, the outermost widget is what is around the focus, so a
        window's shortcuts go on its outermost widget:

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.column([ui.keys(["Ctrl+S", "Ctrl+Shift+P", "F5"]), ui.onKey(Msg.Shortcut(_))], [
        	ui.input([ui.keys(["ArrowUp", "ArrowDown"]), ui.onKey(Msg.History(_))], state.line),
        	ui.textarea([ui.keys(state.mode.claimed), ui.onKey(Msg.Normal(_))], state.text)
        ])
        ~CODE~

        **A key is named by what it types when nothing but Shift is held** — ~BT~"a"~BT~,
        ~BT~"A"~BT~, ~BT~":"~BT~ — and **by the key itself, modifiers first, when Ctrl or Alt
        is**: ~BT~"Ctrl+S"~BT~, ~BT~"Ctrl+Shift+P"~BT~, ~BT~"Ctrl+Alt+O"~BT~, ~BT~"Ctrl+,"~BT~. Keys
        that type nothing have names of their own, with Shift in front where it is held:
        ~BT~Enter~BT~ ~BT~Escape~BT~ ~BT~Tab~BT~ ~BT~Backspace~BT~ ~BT~Delete~BT~ ~BT~Insert~BT~ ~BT~Space~BT~
        ~BT~ArrowUp~BT~ ~BT~ArrowDown~BT~ ~BT~ArrowLeft~BT~ ~BT~ArrowRight~BT~ ~BT~Home~BT~ ~BT~End~BT~
        ~BT~PageUp~BT~ ~BT~PageDown~BT~ ~BT~F1~BT~…~BT~F12~BT~ — so ~BT~"Shift+Tab"~BT~.

        ~BT~"Any"~BT~ takes every key, typing included: what an editor with a mode of its
        own wants while that mode is on. The list is state like any other, so a widget
        takes ~BT~"Any"~BT~ in one mode and nothing in the next.

        ## The pointer's own events

        ~BT~onMenu~BT~, ~BT~onMiddle~BT~, ~BT~onDouble~BT~, ~BT~onPress~BT~, ~BT~onRelease~BT~, ~BT~onWheel~BT~ and
        ~BT~onDrop~BT~ go to **the innermost widget under the pointer that hears them**: a
        right click on a row of a list opens that row's menu, and with an overlay
        anchored at the pointer the menu opens where the click was. ~BT~onDrag(f)~BT~ is
        handed how far the pointer has moved since the press, across and down, as it
        moves and once more when it is let go — so a divider that resizes a panel keeps
        the size it started from and adds the distance:

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.column([ui.height(5), ui.onDrag(Msg.Resize(_, _, _))], [])
        ~CODE~

        **~BT~onEnter~BT~ and ~BT~onLeave~BT~ come in pairs** for every widget the pointer crosses
        that hears them, the innermost first; a widget taken away while the pointer is
        over it is never left — it is gone, and the view that took it knows. **~BT~onFocus~BT~
        and ~BT~onBlur~BT~ are a widget gaining and losing the focus with everything in it**,
        so moving between two fields of one form tells the form nothing; the whole
        window losing the focus to another program is not a widget losing it.

        A widget that hears ~BT~onWheel~BT~ keeps the wheel for itself: what it is in does not
        scroll.

        ## Where the pointer is

        ~BT~onPress~BT~, ~BT~onPoint~BT~ and ~BT~onRelease~BT~ say where, **in pixels from the widget's
        own top left corner — and on a [canvas](ui-canvas.md), in the canvas's own
        units**, so a press on a map is a place on the map. ~BT~onPoint~BT~ is said once a
        frame at most while the pointer moves over the widget, and while a press that
        began on it is held, wherever the pointer goes; ~BT~onRelease~BT~ goes to the widget
        the press began on.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.canvas([ui.region(0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0), ui.onPress(Msg.PlaceAt(_, _))], marks)
        ~CODE~

        ## Carrying and dropping

        ~BT~carry(word)~BT~ lets a widget be **dragged**, and whatever hears ~BT~onDrop~BT~ where it
        is let go is handed the word. What the word means is the program's — an id, a
        name, a position in a list.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.text([ui.carry(card.id)], card.title)
        ui.column([ui.onDrop(Msg.MoveTo(column.id, _))], cards)
        ~CODE~

        ## A file

        ~BT~onFile(f)~BT~ is handed a file's **name**, without its folder, and **its bytes as a
        ~BT~Str~BT~** — the same way [~BT~hive.file~BT~](file.md) reads one, so a picture or an
        archive survives. It is heard from an input of ~BT~kind(InputKind.File)~BT~ when a file
        is chosen, and from **any widget a file is dropped on** from the desktop; a drop
        of several is that many events.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.input([ui.kind(ui.InputKind.File()), ui.onFile(Msg.Uploaded(_, _))], "")
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~onScroll~BT~ and ~BT~onSize~BT~

        ~BT~onScroll~BT~ is said when a box that scrolls has moved, in whole pixels across and
        down. ~BT~onSize~BT~ is said **when a widget first lands and whenever its size
        changes, and at no other time** — keep it in the model, because a model that
        loses it is not told again until the widget moves.

        ## There is no command

        The message goes to ~BT~update~BT~, which is [a service's handler](syslink.md): it writes
        the state, and what it returns is read only by a request from another node.
        **Work that has to happen goes to a service** — a window has an address, handed
        to an ~BT~update~BT~ that takes one third, so anything with something to say posts a
        message back to it when it has one.
    `)
}

func enums(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ — the enumerations

        Ordinary closed types, reached the way every other type is, and **called with
        ~BT~()~BT~ like any other variant**: ~BT~ui.Align.Center()~BT~, not ~BT~ui.Align.Center~BT~.

        **None is an atom, deliberately**: the atom table belongs to your program, and a
        library that added to it would renumber the atoms you wrote.

        | type | variants |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Align~BT~ | ~BT~Start~BT~ ~BT~Center~BT~ ~BT~End~BT~ ~BT~Stretch~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Justify~BT~ | ~BT~Start~BT~ ~BT~Center~BT~ ~BT~End~BT~ ~BT~Between~BT~ |
        | ~BT~TextSize~BT~ | ~BT~Title~BT~ ~BT~Subtitle~BT~ ~BT~Body~BT~ ~BT~Caption~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Axis~BT~ | ~BT~Horizontal~BT~ ~BT~Vertical~BT~ ~BT~Both~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Anchor~BT~ | ~BT~Pointer~BT~ ~BT~Caret~BT~ — where an [overlay](ui-widgets.md) opens |
        | ~BT~InputKind~BT~ | ~BT~Text~BT~ ~BT~Number~BT~ ~BT~Date~BT~ ~BT~Password~BT~ ~BT~Search~BT~ ~BT~Range~BT~ ~BT~Time~BT~ ~BT~DateTime~BT~ ~BT~Email~BT~ ~BT~Tel~BT~ ~BT~Url~BT~ ~BT~Color~BT~ ~BT~File~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Fit~BT~ | ~BT~Fill~BT~ ~BT~Contain~BT~ ~BT~Cover~BT~ — how a picture fills its box |
        | [~BT~Icon~BT~](ui-icons.md) | 320 of them, from ~BT~Search~BT~ to ~BT~Fish~BT~ |
        | [~BT~Tone~BT~](ui-colour.md) | ~BT~Normal~BT~ ~BT~Muted~BT~ ~BT~Good~BT~ ~BT~Warn~BT~ ~BT~Danger~BT~ ~BT~HEX(Str)~BT~ ~BT~RGBA(Int, Int, Int, Int)~BT~ |
        | [~BT~Surface~BT~](ui-scene-surfaces.md) | nineteen finishes |
        | [~BT~Voice~BT~](ui-scene-sound.md) | ~BT~Engine~BT~ ~BT~Tyres~BT~ ~BT~Brakes~BT~ ~BT~Wind~BT~ ~BT~Crowd~BT~ ~BT~Music~BT~ ~BT~Impact~BT~ ~BT~Chime~BT~ ~BT~Talk~BT~ |

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "TURN 7")
        ui.input([ui.kind(ui.InputKind.Password())], state.secret)
        ui.icon([], ui.Icon.Trash())
        ui.column([ui.scroll(ui.Axis.Both())], rows)
        ~CODE~

        The order above is **declaration order**, which is also the order ~BT~sort~BT~ puts
        them in and the order a ~BT~==~BT~ comparison rests on.

        **The icon list is closed and that is the whole of it.** There is no name to
        pass and no file to point at: an icon that is not on [the list](ui-icons.md) is a
        ~BT~text~BT~ or an ~BT~image~BT~.

        **An ~BT~InputKind~BT~ says what a field is for**: ~BT~Time~BT~ and ~BT~DateTime~BT~ get a
        picker, as ~BT~Date~BT~ does; ~BT~Email~BT~, ~BT~Tel~BT~ and ~BT~Url~BT~ get the keyboard a phone shows
        for them and are otherwise text; ~BT~Color~BT~ is a swatch whose value is ~BT~#rrggbb~BT~;
        ~BT~File~BT~ is a button that opens a chooser, heard through
        [~BT~onFile~BT~](ui-events.md#a-file) rather than ~BT~onInput~BT~.

        Being ordinary types, they are matched like any other — ~BT~if chosen is ui.Align.End~BT~
        — and a program that stores one stores a ~BT~hive.ui.Align~BT~.
    `)
}

func icons(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ — the icons

        ~BT~icon(attrs, name)~BT~ draws one of these, **in the colour and at the size of the text
        around it**: ~BT~tone~BT~ colours it and ~BT~size~BT~ sizes it, and in a
        [paragraph](ui-widgets.md#a-paragraph) it stands in the line as a letter would.

        ~CODE~hive
        ui.row([ui.gap(6), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [ui.icon([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], ui.Icon.CircleCheck()),
        	ui.text([], "saved")])
        ~CODE~

        They are drawn from [Lucide](https://lucide.dev), the same lines on a page and in a
        window, and **the list is closed**: what is not here is a ~BT~text~BT~ or an ~BT~image~BT~.

        | | | | | | |
        | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Search~BT~ | ~BT~Close~BT~ | ~BT~Check~BT~ | ~BT~Plus~BT~ | ~BT~Minus~BT~ | ~BT~Up~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Down~BT~ | ~BT~Left~BT~ | ~BT~Right~BT~ | ~BT~Warning~BT~ | ~BT~Info~BT~ | ~BT~Star~BT~ |
